<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portlaoise</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maryborough</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Portlaoise is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">County Laois</a> in the Irish midlands. It grew from a fort built in the 1540s to subdue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omore">O'Mores</a> and was named Maryborough after Mary I when the plantation of Queen's County began in 1556. The town took its present name after Irish independence and is known for its prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portlaoise.jpg" alt="Portlaoise" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Street,_Portlaoise,_2021-07-21,_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Annals of the Four Masters, written in the 1630s, call the site Port Laoighisi. The town grew around the Fort of Leix, or Fort Protector, whose construction began in 1548 under the Lord Deputy, Sir Edward Bellingham, to secure English control of the county after the exile of Gaelic chieftains the year before. The fort stood on rising ground, protected to the south and east by the River Triogue and an esker known as the Ridge.</p><p>An Act of Parliament in the reign of Mary I established the town proper, named Maryborough in her honour, and the county became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">Queen's County</a>. The area had been a focus of the rebellion of Ruairí Óg Ó Mórdha, who lost his lands, which the Crown wanted settled by reliable landowners. For some fifty years the settlers fought a continual low-scale war against Gaelic chieftains who opposed the settlement, and the town was burnt several times by the end of the 16th century.</p><p>Maryborough received a market in 1567, and in 1570 a charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> raised it to a borough. It sent two members to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> until 1800. Its Court House was built in 1805 and its County Gaol in 1830. In 1929, a few years after the foundation of the Irish Free State, the town was renamed Portlaoighise, later simplified to Port Laoise, and the county became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">County Laois</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Portlaoise is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-laois">County Laois</a>, in the South Midlands of the province of Leinster, 94 km west-southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> on the M7 motorway. The Slieve Bloom mountains lie to the west and the Great Heath of Maryborough to the east. It had 23,494 inhabitants at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Remains of Fort Protector can still be seen in the town centre. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplane</a> made in Ireland was assembled in the town, and its first flight was reported on 4 November 1909.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portlaoise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Mary I the English government confiscated Laois and Offaly and in 1556 created Queen's County, planted with English settlers around <b>Maryborough</b>, today Portlaoise.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-loigis">Kingdom of Loigis</a></li></ul></section>
